  /* THE SEARCHED PLACE, AND THE COLOUR THAT WAS NOT AS FREE AS I CLAIMED.
     The note under markHit() said amber «because the access scale does not
     use it». The access scale has `mixed: #E8792B`, which is the same hue as
     the #E8912B ring to within a few degrees — so the marker meaning «this is
     the one you searched for» looked like the marker meaning «partly free,
     partly paid», and on a green basemap dotted with amber archive triangles
     it did not separate from them at all. David: «could the pulsing marker
     background be red or another vibrant colour? its grey really isnt good».

     Red is taken too — `paid` is #C0392B. So is green (free, account),
     yellow (index), purple (catalogue) and grey (onsite, unsurveyed). What
     the scale has never used is magenta, which is also the most vibrant thing
     available against OpenStreetMap's greens and greys.

     THE GREY UNDERNEATH IS REAL AND STAYS. The dot keeps its own access
     colour — grey there means «nobody has walked this one yet», which is
     information, not a rendering fault. What changes is that the ring and its
     wash are now emphatic enough that the marker reads as «found» before the
